## 01 What is R?

R is free open-source software environment created with the idea of offering a complete and advance tool for statistical computing, analysis, and visualization. The language used in this environment is also called R. You can discover more about the R-project at their website (<https://www.r-project.org/>) This language is fully supported by an enormous group of users, communities and groups dedicated to program and develop different tools. It is available at the main operative systems: Windows, MacOS and Linux distributions. For working with R there a different tool, the most used is RStudio that provides a complete free version Integrated Development Environment (IDE). For start working with R, you will need to install on your computer the following tools:

1.  R Language and tools: <https://cran.rediris.es/index.html>
2.  RStudio IDE (desktop): <https://www.rstudio.com/products/rstudio/download/>

In addition, remember that programming is learning by practicing and not memorizing, for these it will be very helpful for you consider the existence of a great amount of cheat sheets that will help you having quick access to the different topics we will cover in the course. Check them here: <https://www.rstudio.com/resources/cheatsheets/>

</br>

## 02 First steps with R

When we open RStudio we will see the different windows. By default when we open it without selecting a R file, we will see the console or terminal, were the output of our code will be displayed and we can write code too, the environment section where we can see the different types of objects we have created and the file explorer also known as workspace, where we can see the different files of our project. We will discuss all these different panels and how to configure it properly in class and review throughout the lessons. In addition we can open a script or other file compatible to edit and execute it.

</br>

Starting with R we can understand it as a calculator, where we can compute different kinds of operations. For example you can write in the console the following code:

```{r, results='hide'}
10 + 5 # Sum
5 - 1 # Sub
10 / 2 # Divide
14 * 3 # Multiply
5 ** 2 # 
2 ^ 10 
```

More complex operations can be calculates like square roots. But this can only be done using build-it functions in R base code. Functions are fragments of code created with the idea use it without the need of writing additional code. We will learn more about functions in next lessons.

Sometimes you will need to save the values you compute, for these in programming we use **variables**. We can compute a complex operation like the example below. And stored in a variable so we don't need to repeat the operation each time and save it for reuse it later.

```{r}
# This is how we declare a variable
pitagoras <- (5**2 + 10**2)

# This how we called it
pitagoras
```

Now we can see this variable in the environment section with the value contained. What happens if we declare the variable again with the same name? What happens if we declare the variable but changing some letter to capital format? What can you do to avoid loosing data when re-declaring the variable again?

Just going deeper, here is an example of variable usage

```{r}
x <- 5**2 # one variable
y <- 10**2 # another variable
pitagoras <- x + y # we can use variables

pitagoras
```

::: box3
