## 03 Datatypes and objects in R

As we learn last section, variable are the way of storing results of operations and other objects. We can find these different types of objects in R.

Each kind of object has his own properties and rules for using them. We can use `class()` build it function to see what kind of object we are working with.

::: box2
**Note 1** When you do not know how to use a function or you need help to understand any type object properties you can use `?` and then write the function at the console, this will display the help section at the panel in RStudio and provide you with all available documentation. Try for example `? class` an see what happens.
:::

### Numeric

Numeric object type is the base format for all type of numbers in R, if we try the `class()` we will see what it returns

```{r}
class(54)
```

As we have seen before, numeric objects can be computed together to make operations.

This type of object is a general definition for numbers, but we can be more detailed when defining it establishing the special numeric type `integer`, for doing this we can add a `L` value to the number.

```{r}
rabbit <- 10L
class(rabbit)
```

This type of object has the same properties as `numeric` but differs on how our computer store it. We are not going to get into special details but is important to know that we can have different types of numeric objects.

### Character

As its names says, characters are objects of type word. Is what we define as a text. For defining a text we just have to write the word or words between this symbols `""`, let's see some examples.

```{r, results='hide'}
text <- "hola"
sentence <- "may the force be with you"
number <- "24"
special.texts <- "@_/&"
```

```{r, results='hold'}
class(text)
class(number)
```

::: box2
**Note 2** As you have seen we have defined a number as a text. It is important to let you know we ***can't*** operate with texts and numbers. Because they don't have the same properties. But you can solve this issue. Try the function `as.numeric()` (remember Note 1 if you don't know how this function works).
:::

### Logical

Logical values are the ones used to establish what we called conditions. In programming they are also known as **Boolean**, so remember this word when you search for information. There are only two types of logical values: `TRUE` and `FALSE`.

Let's see some examples:

```{r, results='hold'}
class(TRUE)
class(FALSE)

bool <- TRUE
class(bool)
```

**How to use them?** The idea behind this type of value is to establish conditions that can be checked by our computer when executing a code. For doing this use special logical operators:

```{r, results='hold'}
"hola" == "hola1" # equal
34 < 24 # lower than
34 > 24 # higher than
34 >= 24 # higher or equal
34 <= 34 # lower or equal
23 != 45 # distinct
```

This will help us to check some conditions prior to execute a piece of code or mark as a flag certain values. We will discover more up next in the lesson.

Take into account, that logical values have their translation to a numeric format. `TRUE` equals to 1 and `FALSE` equals to 0.

```{r, results='hold'}
TRUE == 1
FALSE == 0
```

This will very helpful as we will see in future lessons.

### Vectors

We have seen individual type of objects, but when working with data we will need to manage a great number of them. For these we have vectors, that are what we called an `array` in programming. This objects are a complete set of different objects in a single variable. For defining them we can use `c()` reserved function.

```{r, results='hold'}
numbers <- c(1, 2, 3.23, 4.223, 5.21, 6.6)
words <- c("hotel", "dinosaur", "transport")
booleans <- c(TRUE, FALSE, TRUE, FALSE, TRUE, TRUE)

numbers
words
booleans
```

**What type of object will be those vectors?** Vectors are considered as the type of the objects it contains. Let's see the last three vectors we created.

```{r, results='hold'}
class(numbers)
class(words)
class(booleans)
```

For managing vectors in R, you must remember that they have an index, the position the value occupy in the vector. Vectors in R always start by position R (but this is not common in the rest of programming languages).

::: box3
**Practice 2.** Try creating your own vector and accessing position 0. What happens?
:::

For accessing a value un the vector we need to use `[]` brackets and the position or index desired. We can also use what we called a range in R, this is done using `:` symbol. Let's see some examples.

```{r}
# We will access the word dinosaur
words[2]

# But we can also access to a set of elements
numbers[1:4]
numbers[3:5]

# You can use a vector to establish what indexes you want
indexes <- c(1,3)
words[indexes]
words[-indexes] # Or use for the oposite
```

Finally, you can use logical values too...

```{r}
numbers[booleans] # We reuse the last created vector of logicals
```

::: box2
**Note 3.** There are several functions we can use to explore vectors. One of the most useful ones will be knowing the length of our vector. Try `length()` function for explore the size of your vectors.
:::

</br>

### Special values
Among the basic types we have just learned there are other types that we will encounter when dealing with data. 

Sometimes we can found values that are not informed, in these cases we will see ```NA``` (not available). We will learn how to deal with these values because they are pretty common. Take into account that these values are the same type as basic ones.

```{r, results='hold'}
values <- c(1, 2, NA, 3) # vector

class(values[3])
is.numeric(values[3])
is.na(values[3]) # this is how to find a NA
```

When dealing with numbers we can find situations when a numeric operation can be done and the result is not possible: ```NaN``` (not a number). Also we have representation of infinity ```Inf```. 

Finally, we will study them later in detail, we have factors, that are used for categorical values that have a fixed options. 

```{r}
categories <- c("Villain", "Hero", "Hero", "Neutral", "Villain")

as.factor(categories)
```


### Lists

As vectors lists store multiple elements. We can create a list following this function.

```{r}
my_list <- list(0, 2, 10, 4)
my_list
```

As we see the structure is a bit different. For accessing its elements we need to use `[[]]` notation.

```{r}
my_list[[1]]
```

But here is the cool thing about lists, while vectors need all its values to be the same class (data type), lists can store element of different type.

```{r}
list(1, "two", TRUE, 10, c(1,2,3,4))
```

As you see list can contain whatever we want. Remember functions like `length()` work here too.

### Matrix

We have learned about vectors, but this can be more complex. We can create matrixes, this is combination of vectors. For declaring a matrix we can use the following command.

```{r}
my.matrix <- matrix(c(10, 12, 9, 1, 5, 7, -1, -6, 8, 100, 200, 300), nrow = 3)
my.matrix
```

As we explain in Note 4, when we speak about vectors we talk about length or size. In the case of matrixes, there isn't this concept we use number of columns and rows.

```{r, results='hold'}
ncol(my.matrix)
nrow(my.matrix)
```

When access their elements the basic idea we used for vectors is also available here, but we must consider that there are now two dimensions.

```{r}
my.matrix[2,4] # Exact element row 2 and column 4

my.matrix[1:2,2:3] # Submatrix
```

### Data Frames

Now we will introduce the most important object we will manage when programming for manage data. Not only in R, also in Python, despite properties can change from one language to another.

When we work with data the most common way to store it and handle it is with tables (like matrixes). A Data Frame is a tabular format composed by columns and rows but with a very important differentiation each column can have his own datatype. Let's create our first Data Frame.

```{r}
my.data.frame <- data.frame(
  col1 = 1:4, 
  col2 = c("Apple", "Google", "Microsoft", "Huawei"), 
  col3 = c(3.4, -2.5, 10.1, -0.05), 
  col4 = c(T, F, F, T)
)

my.data.frame
```

When accessing the elements in a data frame we can use the same tools we used with vectors and matrixes.

```{r, results='hold'}
my.data.frame[1,2] # One exact element
my.data.frame[2:4, 2]
my.data.frame[1:3, -4]
```

::: box3
**Practice 3.** Check the types of the returned objects when accessing in the data frame in the three last cases.
:::

But here we have more complex tools, we can access an specific column. Use `$` symbol and write the name of the column or use double brackets `[[]]` and write with `''` the name of the column.

```{r}
# Method 1
my.data.frame$col2 

# Method 2
my.data.frame[['col2']]

```

::: box3
**Practice 4.** Once you have created your first data frame try the following things to learn more about this structure.

-   Use `class()` to see the type of the whole object.
-   Use `class()` to see the type of each column.
:::

From now on we will leave here this explanation about data frames. But these type of object are the most relevant ones we will abroad in the course. We will discuss next lesson this object and how to manipulate it properly.

