## 04 Basic programming structures

When programming there are several predefined structures we must know. These structures will help us approach the different problems we must solve and build our code.

### Conditional structures

We have explained logical object type and the way logical values are used, this structure is the one that will use directly to order the way our code will be executed.

Let's see the general skeleton of these structure:

```{r}
x <- 3
if (x > 2) { 
   print("x is greater than 2")
} else {
  print("x is lower or equal than 2")
}
```

The structure can be simplified if we only want to apply a certain rule of condition is fulfilled.

```{r}
y <- 10
if (y > 0 & y < 5) {
  print("y is between 0 and 5 range")
}
```

::: box2
**Note 4.** At the example above you have seen we write a multiple condition. This is done to check different aspects prior to enter the block of code. There are two types of operators for establishing multiple conditions:

-   AND (`&`), this operator will check that all conditions are accomplished. If one of them fails it won't enter the block of code.
-   OR (`|`), this operator will check that one of the conditions are accomplished. If one of them succeed it will enter the block of code.
:::

We can add multiple cases when dealing with conditional structures, let's analyze this final example a bit more complex.

```{r}
a <- 10
b <- -3

# %% operator will return the module of a division
# we check if the value is fair
if(a %% 2 == 0 & b %% 2 == 0){ 
  c <- a / 2 - b / 2
} else if(a %% 2 & b < 0){
  c <- a / 2 - b
} else {
  c <- a + b
}
c
```

### Iterative structures

The second and last type of structures we will learn are iterative ones. This type of structures are helpful to describe tasks that are repetitive and avoid code repetition. We will learn two types of iterative structures that are called loops.

#### For Loop

This kinf of loop is helpful to run a complete vector of elements or data frame. Let's see an example.

```{r}
for (val in 1:5)
{
    print(val)
}
```

```{r}
# days of the week
week <- c('Sunday',
           'Monday',
           'Tuesday',
           'Wednesday',
           'Thursday',
           'Friday',
           'Saturday')
 
# over each string in the vector
for (day in week)
{
    print(day)
}
```

#### While Loop

In addition we can find a while loop, that works with conditions. This loop will check the condition and if it is accomplished will do more iterations.

```{r}
val <- 1

while (val <= 5) {
  print(val)
  val = val+1
}
val
```

::: box2
**Note 5.** Note that in the case of while loops we need to establish the condition correctly, if we establish a condition that will not be never accomplished we will create an infinity loop, that will cause our computer to crash.

We can also terminate abruptly an execution of a in iterative structure using `break` clause. This will terminate exit the loop.
:::

</br>

