## 05 Libraries

What we've seen so far belongs to what it is known as R base. When programming in R, we'll use a set of words and symbols that the computer understands and operates according to them. But because of the complexity of the projects that are usually developed in Data Science, programming everything in R base may become verbose, difficult and the least practical way of working.

Several people from the R community work continously developing sets of code that, with new words and symbols, expand R and its uses. These sets of code are called libraries or packages (not only in R but in every programming language, such as Python).

For using a library, we employ the function `library()`, with the name of the desired library as the parameter. But first, we will need to install it.

For installing a library we need to type the following command `install.packages()`. For example we are going to work with the `tibble` library that helps us workd with tibble structures, pretty similar to data frames.

```{r, eval=FALSE}
# First we need to install it
install.packages("tibble")
```

Once the library is installed we can call it, we used to do this a the beginning of the script. And then we can use the functions, objects and other functionalities the library has.

```{r}
library(tibble)

tibble(
  my_cool_column = c(23, 56, 77), 
  my_lovely_column = c("thing1", "thing2", "thing3")
)
```

::: box2
**Note 6.** It is important when installing libraries you do it on the console and never write the installation command on the script. Once you have installed the library you don't need to install it again on your machine.

In the other hand remember to write all libraries and dependencies commands at the beginning of your script this will help programmert see what are the dependencies of your script and install it if necessary.
:::

</br>

The key concept we must know here is the necessity of libraries inside R, because R base has limited tools and other programmer and developers continue to create amazing repositories for help in they use in or Data projects.

</br>

## 06 Types of files in the R environment

Before ending this unit we will make a quick review of the different types of files you can work with:

-   R Scripts (file.R): This is the base file with R code, you can execute and create a whole data processing code and directly execute it.
-   R markdown (file.Rmd): Combine both R execution code with markdown comments, we will learn how to use it during lessons and for your assignments.
-   R notebook (file.Rmd): Similar to R markdown, we will learn how to use it during lessons and for your assignments.
- Quarto files (file.qmd): Quarto is the next step in R markdown. It combines the markdown basics with new features to develop websites, pdf and presentations, among others. It can be use with python too and other languages. 


:::box2
**Note 6.** As you see markdown is a basic feature in terms of using R (and Python too). It is basic skill for developing automated reports among other things. You can start learning markdown [here](https://agea.github.io/tutorial.md/).
:::

